  <revision rev="36" vrev="2">
    <srcmd5>6047e07596daea4e856ee698b69b74ee</srcmd5>
    <version>1.3.3</version>
    <time>1786978533</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>thin-provisioning-tools: vendored-dependency refresh, licence re-derivation, and a test multibuild flavour

No version bump - this is 1.3.3 throughout. Three packaging changes.

1. Vendored dependency refresh
-----------------------------
Re-ran the source services, so cargo_vendor re-resolved. The upstream
source tarball is byte-identical (md5 e476697bbe99b50b5bcbb8e84441f222
before and after); only vendor.tar.zst changed. Note the _service sets
update=&quot;true&quot; on cargo_vendor, so the vendored tree deliberately does
not match upstream's committed Cargo.lock - that is intended and is not
something to &quot;correct&quot;.

The tree stayed at 212 crate directories, 66 of which were replaced:
62 crates changed version in place, two were added (jiff-core 0.1.0,
&quot;Unlicense OR MIT&quot;; palette_math 0.7.7, &quot;MIT OR Apache-2.0&quot;) and three
dropped (fast-srgb8, proc-macro-error2, proc-macro-error-attr2). Five
crossed a major boundary: darling, darling_core and darling_macro
0.23.0 -&gt; 0.24.0, env_filter 1.0.1 -&gt; 2.0.0, and syn 2.0.118 -&gt; 3.0.3
(syn 2.x stays in the tree alongside 3.x). No new licence identifier
entered the tree, and neither added crate contributes one that was not
already present; CC0-1.0 left the tree with fast-srgb8.

The refresh broke two tests, which is worth spelling out because it is
the whole point of auditing a re-vendor rather than trusting it. clap
went 4.6.1 -&gt; 4.6.6, and clap 4.6.2 changed its help renderer to say
&quot;alias&quot; instead of &quot;aliases&quot; when an option has exactly one alias (clap
changelog, under Fixes: &quot;(help) Say `alias` when there is only one&quot;).
tests/thin_delta.rs hardcodes the expected --help text, so accepts_h and
accepts_help both failed. Upstream CI never sees this because it builds
from the committed Cargo.lock; we re-resolve, so we do.

Fixed by thin-provisioning-tools-tests-clap-single-alias.patch, which
touches nothing but the two expected strings in that test file - no
non-test code, so the shipped binary is unaffected. Sent upstream as
gh#jthornber/thin-provisioning-tools#328 and referenced from the Patch0
comment; drop the patch once it is merged and released.

2. Licence re-derivation
------------------------
The tag was a bare GPL-3.0-only, which described upstream's own code but
not the shipped artifact: every tool in %files is a symlink to one
statically linked Rust binary (pdata_tools), so the binary also contains
the vendored crates.

Method, reproducible from the tree in this request:
  - Enumerated the licence of all 212 crate directories in the refreshed
    vendor.tar.zst by reading each crate's Cargo.toml. All 212 declare a
    license field; none is license-file-only, so no file had to be
    identified by hand.
  - Scoped to what is actually linked with
    &quot;cargo tree --offline -p thinp -e normal&quot; - 77 of the 212. The build
    is default-features (upstream's Makefile does a plain
    &quot;cargo build --release&quot;), so ratatui/termion/io-uring are out.
  - Elected MIT wherever a crate offers a choice. MIT is on offer from
    every dual/multi-licensed crate in the linked set, so one election
    covers all of them and no Zlib, 0BSD, BSD-2-Clause, Unlicense or
    BSL-1.0 obligation is taken on. Only the elected branch is declared.

What is left mandatory, and therefore declared:
  GPL-3.0-only  upstream itself (COPYING)
  MIT           12 crates that offer nothing else - console,
                data-encoding, indicatif, libudev-sys, nix, nom,
                quick-xml, retry, simd-adler32, strsim, udev,
                unit-prefix - plus every crate where MIT was elected
  Apache-2.0    exitcode 1.1.2, which offers no alternative
  MPL-2.0       devicemapper 0.34.8 and devicemapper-sys 0.3.3, reached
                through thin_migrate
  Unicode-3.0   unicode-ident 1.0.24, whose expression is an AND

  License: Apache-2.0 AND GPL-3.0-only AND MIT AND MPL-2.0 AND Unicode-3.0

Deliberately excluded, and why: build-only and dev-only crates are not in
the binary. That drops bindgen (BSD-3-Clause), clang-sys (Apache-2.0) and
libloading (ISC), which devicemapper-sys uses only to generate FFI
bindings at build time, and r-efi, whose LGPL-2.1-or-later option is
UEFI-target-only and absent from the Linux graph. Every identifier used
was checked against the openSUSE accepted-licence list, so nothing had to
be omitted for validity.

Compatibility, raised rather than silently assumed: GPL-3.0-only combined
with Apache-2.0 vendored code is a real question, so to be explicit -
Apache-2.0 and MPL-2.0 are each one-way compatible with GPLv3, so the
combined binary is distributable and is effectively GPL-3.0-only as a
whole. The reverse direction is not claimed. MPL-2.0 section 3.2 source
availability is satisfied because the complete vendor.tar.zst ships in
the src.rpm.

The derivation is recorded as a # Legal-Review-Notice block above the
License: tag so a reviewer can re-run it without redoing the audit.

Because MIT and Apache-2.0 both require their notice to travel with the
binary, and upstream's COPYING covers only the GPL-3.0 part, the licence
text of each linked crate that ships one is now installed under
%{_defaultlicensedir} (149 files, 722 KiB). The list is derived at build
time from cargo tree, so it cannot go stale across a re-vendor. crc32c
0.6.8 declares MIT OR Apache-2.0 but ships no licence file of its own.

3. %check split into a &quot;test&quot; multibuild flavour
------------------------------------------------
Measured on this package: %check was 267s of a 376s build - 71% of the
total. cargo test spends 50s recompiling in the test profile and one test
block alone takes 186s. 88 packages build-depend on thin-provisioning-tools,
so a test flake stalls a large fan-out.

Mechanics follow the ruff / uv / zig precedent, keeping ONE spec file:
_multibuild with a single &lt;flavor&gt;test&lt;/flavor&gt;; %global flavor
@BUILD_FLAVOR@%{nil} with the %else branch defining the suffix as %{nil},
since obs-build substitutes @BUILD_FLAVOR@ textually. The %package-level
declarations are guarded, not just %files - OBS reads them when it
schedules. The test flavour therefore produces only a src.rpm and runs
%{cargo_test}; the main flavour no longer has a %check.

The test flavour also has no %build: cargo test compiles in the test
profile anyway, so running %{cargo_build} first would only add a
throwaway release compile.

Staging will need an excludebuild:thin-provisioning-tools:test line. That
is a release-team action; this request does not carry it.

Gates
-----
Both flavours built green locally on aarch64 (native) against
Base:System/openSUSE_Factory.

  main flavour: finished &quot;build thin-provisioning-tools.spec&quot;; rpmlint
                2 packages checked, 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 badness.
  test flavour: finished; 25 test blocks, 598 tests passed, 0 failed -
                the same 25/598 the pre-split OBS build produced, so the
                suite really ran and nothing was silently skipped. It
                emits only thin-provisioning-tools-test-1.3.3-0.src.rpm
                and no binary package, and the log shows zero
                target/release compiles, confirming the release build is
                genuinely skipped there.

Main-flavour RPM content proven unchanged by the restructure, against the
published thin-provisioning-tools-1.3.3-88.9.aarch64.rpm:
  - file list, excluding the newly added vendored licence texts:
    IDENTICAL, all 49 paths byte for byte. 226 paths were added, every
    one under /usr/share/licenses/thin-provisioning-tools/vendor; 0
    added anywhere else, 0 removed.
  - provides, excluding bundled(rust-crate:*): identical apart from the
    release number (1.3.3-88.9 published vs 1.3.3-0 for a local build).
    The bundled(rust-crate:*) set is the vendor refresh itself.
  - requires: identical except for one added
    rpmlib(PartialHardlinkSets), which is %fdupes hardlinking the
    duplicate licence texts.
  So the restructure itself changes nothing; both deltas belong to
  changes 1 and 2 and are individually accounted for.

source_validator clean; changes-lint and changes-guard clean;
spec-cleaner --remove-groups --pkgconfig --perl --tex diff empty.

No open openSUSE bugs reference this package, so nothing is cited.</comment>
